Sval: a lazy tab given new state reports its new address

SessionStore.restoreTab replaces a lazy tab's TAB_LAZY_STATES entry, but the
lazy browser's currentURI getter (tabbrowser's _createLazyBrowser) returns
the URI it cached on first read, so the tab goes on reporting its old address
until its browser is inserted. tabbrowser's own swapBrowsersAndCloseOther
clears that cache before it calls setTabState; this clears it where the state
is replaced, for every caller. Sval points a restored, unloaded tab at its
tree node's address with setTabState, and reads the address back from
currentURI.

diff --git a/browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs b/browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs
index ee556f32c3..af971063bb 100644
--- a/browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs
+++ b/browser/components/sessionstore/SessionStore.sys.mjs
@@ -6658,6 +6658,9 @@ var SessionStoreInternal = {
         userTypedValue: tabData.userTypedValue || "",
         userTypedClear: tabData.userTypedClear || 0,
       });
+      // The lazy browser's currentURI getter caches the URI it last read from
+      // TAB_LAZY_STATES, so it must forget it when that state is replaced.
+      delete browser._cachedCurrentURI;
     }
 
     // Most of tabData has been restored, now continue with restoring
